# 2muchcoffee NestJS standards

Apply these rules to every NestJS change in this repo. Order: architecture, structure, code-level.

## Architecture

- Modularize by feature, not by layer. Every domain is a module under `src/modules/<feature>/`.
- Use dependency injection. Never `new` up a service or repository inside another service.
- Follow NestJS defaults unless you can justify deviation.
- `tsconfig.json` must have `"strict": true`. Refuse to write `any`.
- Prefer decorators over imperative plumbing.

## Project structure

When you generate or move files, follow this shape:

```
src/
├── modules/
│   ├── <feature>/
│   │   ├── <feature>.controller.ts
│   │   ├── <feature>.service.ts
│   │   ├── dto/
│   │   ├── entities/
│   │   └── <feature>.module.ts
│   └── shared/
│       ├── decorators/
│       ├── validators/
│       ├── middlewares/
│       ├── classes/
│       └── shared.module.ts
├── app.module.ts
└── main.ts
```

`SharedModule` is the only cross-module export point. Do not import internals from one feature module into another.

## Controllers

- Keep them thin. Delegate to services.
- Use REST verb decorators correctly: `@Post` create, `@Get` read, `@Put` replace, `@Patch` partial update, `@Delete` remove.
- Use explicit `@HttpCode` when the response is not 200.

```typescript
@Controller('users')
export class UserController {
  constructor(private readonly userService: UserService) {}

  @Post()
  @HttpCode(HttpStatus.CREATED)
  create(@Body() dto: CreateUserDto) {
    return this.userService.create(dto);
  }
}
```

## DTOs and validation

- Every controller input goes through a DTO with `class-validator` decorators.
- Apply the global `ValidationPipe` in `main.ts` with `whitelist: true` and `forbidNonWhitelisted: true`.
- Use enums, never magic strings.

```typescript
export class CreateUserDto {
  @IsString()
  @Length(2, 100)
  name: string;

  @IsEmail()
  email: string;
}
```

## Services

- Business logic lives here.
- Constructor-inject everything.
- Use `async`/`await`. Do not chain `.then()`.
- Public methods are single-responsibility with clear names.

## Imports

Order: NestJS modules, then third-party packages, then local imports. Separate the three groups with a blank line.

## Error handling

- Throw `HttpException` subclasses for known errors (`BadRequestException`, `NotFoundException`, `ConflictException`).
- Never throw plain `Error` from a controller or service.
- Register a global exception filter for response shape consistency.
- Never expose stack traces or internal error details to clients in production.

```typescript
@Catch(HttpException)
export class GlobalExceptionFilter implements ExceptionFilter {
  catch(exception: HttpException, host: ArgumentsHost) {
    const ctx = host.switchToHttp();
    const response = ctx.getResponse<Response>();
    response.status(exception.getStatus()).json({
      statusCode: exception.getStatus(),
      message: exception.message,
    });
  }
}
```

## Common HTTP status codes

200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content, 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict, 422 Unprocessable Entity, 500 Internal Server Error.

## Security

- Always validate input.
- Apply auth guards globally where possible.
- Use Helmet, an explicit CORS allowlist, and rate limiting on every public endpoint.
- Never read, stage, or commit `.env`, `.env.local`, `.env.*`, or any file containing real-looking secrets. If you see one in the working set, stop and ask before continuing.
- Generate environment files from `.env.example` per environment.

## Authentication and authorization

- Stateless token auth.
- Separation of authentication and authorization.
- Access Token short-lived. Refresh Token long-lived and rotated.
- Centralize the auth check.

## Performance and scalability

- Paginate every list endpoint. Default `limit` 20, max `limit` 100.
- Cache reads where the data tolerates it.
- Avoid N+1 queries.
- Enable compression in `main.ts`.

## Testing

- Use Jest. Mock dependencies with `@nestjs/testing`.
- Every public service method gets at least one unit test.
- Every API endpoint gets at least one e2e test covering one happy path and one error path.
- If you skip required tests, say so explicitly in your response.

```typescript
describe('UserService', () => {
  let service: UserService;

  beforeEach(async () => {
    const module = await Test.createTestingModule({
      providers: [UserService],
    }).compile();

    service = module.get<UserService>(UserService);
  });

  it('should be defined', () => {
    expect(service).toBeDefined();
  });
});
```

## Hard refusals

- Do not write `any`.
- Do not commit `.env` files or any file containing real secrets.
- Do not disable validation pipes globally without a replacement guard.
- Do not bypass module boundaries by importing internals from another feature module.
- Do not add a service-level method without at least one unit test, unless the human has explicitly accepted the trade-off in the same turn.
